-- title:
--   Vanishing ideal of an element of a projective module
-- statement:
--   Let $B$ be a commutative ring in a universe $u$, and let $M$ be a finitely generated projective $B$-module (an additive commutative group with a $B$-module structure, in a possibly different universe $v$), and let $x \in M$. The assertion is that there exists an ideal $J \subseteq B$ with the following property: for every commutative ring $A$ whose carrier lies in the same universe $u$ as $B$, and every ring homomorphism $\varphi : B \to A$, regarding $A$ as a $B$-algebra via $\varphi$, the element $1 \otimes x$ of $A \otimes_B M$ is zero if and only if the ideal of $A$ generated by the image $\varphi(J)$, i.e. `Ideal.map φ J`, is the zero ideal. Thus a single ideal $J$ of $B$ detects the vanishing of $x$ after every base change, uniformly in $A$ and $\varphi$. The quantification over $A$ is restricted to the universe of $B$.
--
--   This is the statement that the locus on which a section $x$ of a finitely generated projective module vanishes is a closed subfunctor of $\operatorname{Spec} B$, cut out by one ideal compatibly with arbitrary base change. It is used in the construction of the Grassmannian as a scheme, specifically in [`Module.Grassmannian.exists_scheme_represents_isHopfIdeal_and_isClosedImmersion_toProjSpace`](thm.html#Module.Grassmannian.exists_scheme_represents_isHopfIdeal_and_isClosedImmersion_toProjSpace), to exhibit the relevant conditions as closed conditions.
